360	/**
361	 * One private match (§14): the only writer of its state. It runs the shared simulation on a 60 Hz fixed step,
362	 * applies validated client intent, and mirrors the simulation into the synced schema after every world step.
363	 *
364	 * The match flow (§2, §5): the match module (`stepMatch`) runs the lobby, the host's start, the ready countdown and
365	 * control start, then the end and the results; ships take input only between control start and the end. From control
366	 * start each world tick runs §5's order: the encounters (reservations first), the ledger's holds, the claim channel
367	 * (`stepClaim`), the flow (with the tick's accomplishments and the claim's end), then intel (`stepIntel`, whose reveals
368	 * and leads go to exactly their recipients, with the targeted rumor purchases decided around it and charged only when a
369	 * lead is delivered). A world tick that throws aborts the match (§12). Every system's
370	 * seed is derived from one private master secret per match (`deriveSeed`), never from the match code.
371	 *
372	 * Encounters (§6): each world step the room builds the tick's snapshot for `stepEncounters` (every ship, whether its
373	 * player is connected, pressed the grapple, or has a loaded arena) and applies the events: a landed grapple stops both
374	 * ships and opens a duel at sea, a `DuelMachine` (the standalone duel room's own) whose fight starts when the rules
375	 * say so; its end goes back as a report; the returns and respawns move ships. Reserved and respawning ships get
376	 * neutral input.
377	 *
378	 * Rubbings (§4, §11, §12): the objective ledger is the only writer of rubbings and gold; the room calls its operations
379	 * and never moves either itself. Holding E moored at an island's dock collects its first eligible source, or else
380	 * claims a rubbing from a recovery cache at that dock. A reservation reserves the duel in the ledger; the fight's end
381	 * is reported to it; the room reports `settled` to the encounter rules only when the ledger has settled the duel (the
382	 * winner's theft choice, or the default at the deadline), and only then do the players return. The world tick drives
383	 * every §12 timer: a drop disconnects the player in the ledger at once (the ship stays, and can be grappled), a
384	 * fighter away from an unfinished duel forfeits it after `duelAbsenceTicks`, the seat expires at the ledger's
385	 * deadline into a cache at the respawn dock, and any other leave is a quit into a cache at the nearest dock by sea.
386	 * No path removes a ship while the ledger still counts its player; `checkInvariants` checks that and `checkLedger`
387	 * after every tick.
388	 *
389	 * Fruit (§9): four pedestals at island docks, dealt from the fruit's own seed. A player moored at a pedestal's dock
390	 * inspects it (the kit goes to them only) and eats it by holding F for three seconds; the fruit rules alone decide who
391	 * eats, and each world tick hears this tick's reservations and disconnects. Every duel at sea starts with both
392	 * fighters' kits in its frame-0 state.
393	 *
394	 * Visibility (§16): every client has a Colyseus StateView. After each mirror the room puts into it the client's own
395	 * ship and every ship inside that ship's sight radius, and takes out the rest, so a ship out of sight is never encoded
396	 * into that client's patches. Ships in a duel or waiting to respawn are hidden from everyone else (a duel shows as a
397	 * marker). Grapples, markers and caches are filtered by the same sight rule (a grapple's target is blanked for a
398	 * viewer who cannot see it); a player's status (gold, rubbings, what their island and dock hold, their theft choice)
399	 * and duel go to that player only, and a duel's frames and probes are messages to its two fighters. A fighter or a
400	 * player waiting to respawn sees no other ship. Terrain is static content every client already has, so it is not
401	 * synced at all.
402	 */
